    88	\begin{abstract} 
    89	We obtain a polynomial type upper bound for the size of the integral solutions of
    90	  Thue equations $F(X,Y) = b$ defined over a totally real number field $K$,
    91	assuming  that  $F(X,1)$ has  a  root  $\alpha$ such that
    92	  $K(\alpha)$ is a CM-field.
    93	Furthermore, we give an algorithm for the computation of the integral solutions of  such an equation.
    94	 
    95	 \  \\
    96	 Keywords: Thue equations, integral solutions, CM-fields.\\
    97	 MSC : 11D59; 11Y16 ; 11G30; 11G50.
    98	\end{abstract}
    99	
   100	
   101	 \maketitle
   102	
   103	\section{Introduction}
   104	
   105	
   106	Let $F(X,Y)$ be an irreducible binary form in $\mbox{\Bdd Z}[X,Y]$ with $\deg F \geq 3$ and $b\in\mbox{\Bdd Z} \setminus\{0\}$.
   107	In  1909,  Thue  \cite{Thue}  proved  that  the  equation
   108	$F(X,  Y)  =  b$ has  only  finitely  many    solutions  $(x,  y)\in\mbox{\Bdd Z}^2$.
   109	Thue's  proof  was ineffective and therefore  does not provide  a method to  determine  the integer
   110	solutions of this equation. Other  non effective  proofs  of  Thue's result    can  be  found  in  \cite[Chap.  X]{Dickson}  and  \cite[Chap. 23]{Mordell}.
   111	
   112	
   113	In  1968, Baker \cite{Baker}, using his results on linear forms in logarithms
   114	of algebraic numbers, computed an explicit upper bound for the size of the integer
   115	 solutions of Thue equations. Baker's result were improved by several authors
   116	 (see for instance \cite{Bugeaud}, \cite{Gyory1}, \cite{Poulakis1})
   117	  but the bounds  remain  of exponential  type and thus, are not useful to compute integer solutions of such equations.
   118	Nevertheless, computation techniques for the resolution of Thue equations have been developed based on the above results 
   119	\cite{Bilu}, \cite{Hanrot}, \cite{Petho}, \cite{Tzanakis} and the solutions of certain parameterized families
   120	 of Thue equations have been obtained \cite{Heuberger}.
   121	Furthermore, upper bounds for the number of integral solutions of Thue equations have been given \cite{Brinza}, \cite{Evertse}, \cite{B_P_vdP_W}.
   122	
   123	In the case where all roots of the polynomial $F(X,1)$ are non real, we have a
   124	polynomial type bound provided by other methods \cite[Theorem 2, page 186]{Mordell}, \cite{Gyory} \cite{Poulakis2}.
   125	Gy\H{o}ry's improvement in \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 1]{Gyory} holds in the case where the splitting field of $F(X,1)$ is a CM-field i.e., 
   126	is a totally imaginary quadratic extension of a totally real number field.
   127	In the same paper, Gy\H{o}ry studied Thue equations defined over a CM-field $L$ and also gave  (\cite[Th\'eor\`{e}me 2]{Gyory})
   128	 a polynomial upper bound for the size of their real algebraic integers solutions in $L$.
   129	
   130	In this paper, we consider Thue equations $F(X,  Y)  =  b$ defined over a totally real number field $K$. Simplifying
   131	  Gy\H{o}ry's approach, we obtain (Theorem \ref{thuetheorem}) polynomial type bounds for the size of their integral solutions over $K$,
   132	assuming  that $F(X,1)$ has  a  root  $\alpha$ such that  the  field $K(\alpha)$ is a CM-field.
   133	In case where the splitting field is a CM-field we are in
   134	the situation of \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 2]{Gyory}.
   135	Whenever all  roots of the polynomial $F(X,1)$ are non real and $K\neq \mbox{\Bdd Q}$, we obtain much better bounds than those
   136	 already known \cite{Poulakis2}. Moreover, whenever $F(X,1)$ has a real and a non real root, we obtain  polynomial type bounds  that the Baker's method was
   137	  not able to provide other than exponential bounds. Furthermore, the method of the proof of Theorem 1 provides us with an algorithm 
   138	 for the determination of the solutions of such equations.
   139	
   140	We illustrate our result by giving two examples of infinite families of Thue equations $F(X,  Y)  =  b$ satisfying the hypothesis of
   141	 Theorem \ref{thuetheorem}. First we consider Thue equations over some totally real subfields $K$ of cyclotomic fields  $N$
   142	 such that the splitting field $L$ of $F(X,1)$ over $K$ is contained in $N$. In this case, $L$ is an abelian extension
   143	 of $K$.  Next, we give a family of equations $F(X,Y)=b$ such that $F(X,1)$ has  a  root  $\alpha$ for which   $K(\alpha)$ is 
   144	a biquadratic  CM-field. These families contain equations such that $F(X,1)$ has also real roots and so the only method for having
   145	upper bound for the size of theirs solutions is Baker's method which provide only bounds of exponential type.
   146	Finally, we give two examples of determination of solutions of equations satisfying the hypothesis of Theorem 1, by using our algorithm.
   147	
   148	 
   149	
   150	\section{New bounds}
   151	
   152	We introduce a few notations.
   153	Let $K$ be a number field. We consider the set of absolute values of $K$ by extending  the ordinary absolute value $|\cdot|$ of $\mbox{\Bdd Q}$
   154	and, for every prime $p$, by extending the $p$-adic absolute  value $|\cdot|_{p}$ with $|p|_{p} = p^{-1}$.
   155	Let $M(K)$ be an indexing set of symbols $v$ such that  $|\cdot|_v$, $v\in M(K)$, are all of the
   156	above absolute values of $K$.
   157	 Given such an absolute value $|\cdot|_{v}$ on $K$, we denote by
   158	 $d_{v}$  its local degree. Let ${\bf x} = (x_0:\ldots:x_{n})$ be  a point of the projective space
   159	$\mbox{\Bdd P}^n(K)$ over $K$.  We define the field height $H_{K}(\bf x)$ of $\bf x$ by
   160	$$ H_K({\bf x}) = \prod_{v \in M(K)} \max\{|x_{0}|_{v},\ldots,|x_{n}|_{v}\}^{d_v}.$$
   161	Let $d$ be the degree of $K$. We define  the absolute height $H(\bf x)$ by
   162	$H({\bf x}) = H_K({\bf x})^{1/d}$.
   163	 For $x \in K$, we put $H_K(x) = H_K((1:x))$ and $H(x) = H((1:x))$. If $G \in K[X_1,\ldots,X_m]$,
   164	 then we define the field height $H_{K}(G)$ and the absolute height $H(G)$ of $G$ as
   165	the field height and the absolute height respectively of the point whose coordinates are the coefficients of
   166	$G$ (in any order). For an account of the properties of heights see \cite{Hindry,Lang,Silverman}.
   167	 Furthermore, we denote by $O_K$ and $N_K$ the ring of integers
   168	of $K$ and the norm relative to the extension $K/{\mbox{\Bdd Q}}$, respectively.
   169	Finally, for every $z\in {\mbox{\Bdd C}}$ we denote, as usually, by $\bar{z}$ its complex conjugate.
   170	
   171	We prove the following theorem:
   172	
   173	
   174	 \begin{myth}\label{thuetheorem}
   175	 Let $K$ be  a totally real number field of degree $d$.
   176	 Let $b\in O_K\setminus \{0\}$ and  $F(X,Y)\in O_K[X,Y]$ be a form of degree $n \geq 2$.
   177	Suppose that $F(X,1)$ has   a  root $\alpha$ such that  $K(\alpha)$ is a CM-field.
   178	Then the solutions $(x,y)\in O_K^2$ of $F(X,Y)=b$ satisfy
   179	$$H(x) <  \Omega_1 \ \ {\rm and}\ \  H(y) < \Omega_2$$
   180	for the following values of $\Omega_1$ and $\Omega_2$.
   181	If  the coefficients of $X^n$ and $Y^n$ are $\pm 1$, then
   182	$$\Omega_1 = \Omega_2 =   32  H(b)^{1/n} H(F)^{1+1/n} N_{K}(b)^{2/d}.$$
   183	If only the coefficient of $X^n$ is $\pm 1$, then
   184	$$\Omega_1 = 2^9   H(b)^{1/n} H(F)^{2+1/n} N_{K}(b)^{4/d} \ \  {\rm and} \ \  \Omega_2 = 32  H(b)^{1/n} H(F)^{1+1/n} N_{K}(b)^{2/d}.$$
   185	If both the coefficients of $X^n$ and $Y^n$ are $ \neq \pm 1$, then 
   186	$$\Omega_1 =   2^9   H(b)^{1/n} H(\Gamma)^{2n+1} N_{K}(b)^{4/d} H(a_0)^{n-1} N_K(a_0)^{2(n-1)/d}$$ 
   187	and
   188	$$\Omega_2 =  32  H(b)^{1/n} H(\Gamma)^{n+1} N_{K}(b)^{2/d} H(a_0)^{n-1} N_K(a_0)^{2(n-1)/d},$$
   189	where $a_0$ is the coefficient of $X^n$ and $\Gamma$  a point of the projective space with  $1$ and the coefficients of $F(X,Y)$ as coordinates. 
   190	Furthermore, the  number of integral solutions to the equation $F(X,Y) = b$ is at most 
   191	$$ 72\cdot 4^{dn} N_K(b)^{2n}.$$
   192	In case where  $b$ is a unit of $O_K$,  this number is at most $2wn$, where $w$ is  the number of the roots of unity in $K(\alpha)$. 
   193	\end{myth}
   194	
   195	The proof of this result is relied on the following property of CM-fields. A non real algebraic number field $L$ is a CM-field if and only if $L$
   196	is closed under the operation of complex conjugation and complex conjugation
   197	commutes with all the $\mbox{\Bdd Q}$-monomorphisms of $L$ into $\mbox{\Bdd C}$ (\cite{Blanksby}, \cite[Th\'{e}or\`{e}me 1]{Gyory2}, \cite[Lemma 2]{Lou_Oka_Oli}).
   198	
   199	When $K =\mbox{\Bdd Q}$ and the splitting field of $F(X,1)$ over $\mbox{\Bdd Q}$ is an abelian totally imaginary extension,  the  hypothesis on complex 
   200	conjugation is obviously satisfied. If  the coefficient of $X^n$ is $\pm 1$, it is interesting to notice  that our bounds are essentially independent
   201	 of the degree of the  form  $F(X,Y)$.
   202	Thus, in case where $H(F)$ and $H(b)$ are not too large, an exhaustive search can provide the  integer solutions we are looking for.
   203	
   204	Finally, it should be noticed that in case where $F(X,Y)$ is irreducible we are  in
   205	the situation of \cite{Gyory}. For $K = \mbox{\Bdd Q}$,  \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 1]{Gyory} provides a better upper bound than Theorem 1 and for 
   206	 $K \neq \mbox{\Bdd Q}$,  \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 2]{Gyory} gives similar 
   207	 upper bounds to  Theorem 1. Suppose $F(X,Y)$ is reducible. If   $K = \mbox{\Bdd Q}$ and $F_1(X,Y)$ is a non trivial irreducible factor of $F(X,Y)$
   208	 over $\mbox{\Bdd Z}$ such that the splitting field of $F_1(X,1)$ is of CM-type, then  each integer solution 
   209	$(x,y)$   of  $F(X,Y) = b$ satisfies $F_1(x,y) = b_1$, for some divisor $b_1$ of  $b$. Thus, \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 1]{Gyory} 
   210	applies to this equation and gives better 
   211	explicit bound for $|x|$ and $|y|$ than Theorem 1. Finally, let  $K \neq \mbox{\Bdd Q}$ and   $F_1(X,Y)$ be 
   212	 a non trivial irreducible factor  of $F(X,Y)$ over $O_K$ of degree $\nu$ such that the splitting field of $F_1(X,1)$ is of CM-type. Then each solution 
   213	$(x,y)\in O_K^2$   of  $F(X,Y) = b$ satisfies $F_1(x,y) = b_1$, for some divisor $b_1$ of  $b$. Note that  we do not know the height of $b_1$.
   214	For this we use  \cite[Lemma 3]{Gyory1} which yields a unit $\epsilon \in O_k$ having 
   215	$$H(b_1\epsilon^{\nu}) \leq N_K(b_1)^{1/d} \exp\{c\nu R_K\},$$
   216	 where $c$ is an explicit constant and $R_K$ the regulator of $K$. Thus, we have
   217	 $F_1(\epsilon x,\epsilon y) = b_1\epsilon^{\nu}$ and so, using \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 2]{Gyory}, we obtain an upper bound for $H(x)$ and $H(y)$
   218	 with an  extra factor which is exponential in terms $R_K$  and hence it  is clearly worse than that of Theorem 1.
   219	 
   220	 
   221	
   222	\section{Examples}
   223	
   224	In this section we give two examples in order to illustrate  our result. We denote by $F^*(X,Y)$ the homogenization of a polynomial
   225	$F(X)\in {\mbox{\Bdd C}}[X]$.
   226	
   227	\begin{myex}{\rm
   228	Let $p$ be a prime with $p \equiv 1 \ (\bmod\,4)$ and $\zeta_p$ a  $p$-th
   229	primitive root of unity in $\mbox{\Bdd C}$. Then the quadratic
   230	field ${\mbox{\Bdd Q}}(\sqrt{p})$ is a subfield of $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta_p)$. The field $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta_p)$ is a cyclic extension of $\mbox{\Bdd Q}$
   231	with Galois group  $\Gal(\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta_p)/\mbox{\Bdd Q}) \simeq (\mbox{\Bdd Z}/p\mbox{\Bdd Z})^*$.
   232	
   233	Let $\alpha\in\mbox{\Bdd Z}[\zeta_p]$ be a primitive element of the extension $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta)/\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{p})$ and
   234	  $\alpha_1,\ldots, \alpha_m$, with
   235	$m = (p-1)/2$,  all the distinct conjugates of $\alpha$ over $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{p})$.
   236	The largest real field contained in $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta_p)$ is $K_p =\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta_p+\bar{\zeta}_p)$ which is a totally real number field.
   237	Let  $\beta\in K_p$ be a primitive element of the extension $K_p/\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{p})$ and $\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_n$, where $n = (p-1)/4$,
   238	all the distinct conjugates of $\beta$ over $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{p})$. Then the polynomial
   239	$$F(X) = (X-\alpha_1)\cdots (X-\alpha_m)(X-\beta_1)\cdots (X-\beta_n)$$
   240	belongs to $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{p})[X]$ and has real and non real roots.  Furthermore, we have $\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{p})(a_i) = \mbox{\Bdd Q}(\zeta_p)$
   241	which is a CM-field. Consequently, for every non zero
   242	$b\in\mbox{\Bdd Z}[(1+\sqrt{p})/2]$, the Thue equation $F^*(X,Y)=b$ satisfies the hypothesis of  Theorem \ref{thuetheorem}. Note that this equation
   243	satisfies also the hypothesis of \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 2]{Gyory}.
   244	
   245	Then, using \cite[Theorem 5.9, page 211]{Silverman} and \cite[Lemma 5.10, page 213]{Silverman},  Theorem \ref{thuetheorem} gives
   246	 the following upper bound
   247	 for the heights of  solutions  $x,y\in\mbox{\Bdd Z}[(1+\sqrt{p})/2]$:
   248	 $$H(x) < 2^{(3p+17)/4} ( H(\alpha)^2H(\beta))^{(3p+1)/6}    H(b)^{4/3(p-1)} N_{\mbox{\footnotesizeBdd Q}(\sqrt{p})}(b)$$
   249	 and
   250	 $$H(y) < 2^{(3p+13)/2}  ( H(\alpha)^2H(\beta))^{5(p-1)/6} H(b)^{4/3(p-1)} N_{\mbox{\footnotesizeBdd Q}(\sqrt{p})}(b)^2.$$
   251	If we consider the particular case where $\Phi_p(X)$ is the $p$-th cyclotomic polynomial, then  \cite[Section 2]{Gyory}
   252	 implies that the maximum of the absolute  heights of
   253	all algebraic integers $x,y \in K_p$ with  $\Phi_p^*(x,y) = 1$  is
   254	$< 2^{(p-1)/2}$.  Theorem  1 improves this result by  yielding the bound  $32$. 
   255	}
   256	\end{myex}
   257	
   258	\begin{myex}
   259	{ \rm  Let $d$ be a positive integer $\geq 2$ and $r=m+n\sqrt{d}$, where $m$, $n$ are  integers such that $m>0$ and  $m^2-n^2d>0$.
   260	The minimal polynomial of $ r$ over $\mbox{\Bdd Q}$ is
   261	$$M(X) = X^2-2mX+m^2-dn^2.$$
   262	Then,  the polynomial
   263	$$P(X)= M(-X^2)= X^4+2mX^2+m^2-dn^2$$
   264	is the minimal polynomial of $\sqrt{-r}$  over $\mbox{\Bdd Q}$. Since $m>0$ and $m^2-n^2d>0$, their roots are not real and so, 
   265	$\mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{d},\sqrt{-r})$ is a CM-field. If $Q(X) \in \mbox{\Bdd Z}[\sqrt{d}][X]\setminus \mbox{\Bdd Z}$, then we put 
   266	$$F(X) = (X^2+(m+n\sqrt{d})Y^2) Q(X).$$
   267	Then for every nonzero $b \in \mbox{\Bdd Z}[\sqrt{d}]$, the Thue equation $F^*(X,Y) = b$  over $K = \mbox{\Bdd Q}(\sqrt{d})$
   268	 satisfies the hypothesis of Theorem 1.  Suppose that $Q(X)$ is monic and $\deg Q = q >0$. By \cite[Remark B.7.4]{Hindry}, we have
   269	 $$H(F) \leq 4H(m+n\sqrt{d})H(Q).$$
   270	 Thus Theorem 1 yields the following upper bounds for the height of integral solutions of the above equations over $K$:
   271	 $$H(x) <     2^{13+1/q}   H(b)^{1/2q} (H(m+n\sqrt{d})H(Q))^{2+1/q} N_{K}(b)^2,$$
   272	 $$H(y) <     2^{7+1/q}  H(b)^{1/2q} (H(m+n\sqrt{d})H(Q))^{1+1/q} N_K(b).$$
   273	Note that in case where the splitting field of $F(X)$ is not a CM-field, \cite[Th\'eor\`eme 2]{Gyory} cannot be applied. Furthermore,
   274	Baker's method can provide only bounds of exponential type.
   275	
   276	}
   277	\end{myex}
